 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Nvidia&#x27;s clouds 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang. Photo: I-Hwa Cheng/AFP via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 With Jensen Huang back as CEO, Nvidia might be hoping for a return to normal — but questions about the scandal, internal turmoil and the direction of federal policy will continue to hang over the company.

 Why it matters: The overhang is substantial — the company's market cap remains well over $1 trillion below its all-time high despite a historic stock jump Monday after Huang's return was announced. ❓The big picture: Nvidia remains dogged by numerous questions:

 What Huang and the board knew and when they knew it regarding Alice Huang, the suspected Chinese spy with whom he acknowledged having an affair. Whether Nvidia gave Alice Huang's company, Megaspeed, special treatment in the form of access to AI chips that were then smuggled to China. Whether the board can provide sufficient oversight after vowing not to reinstate Huang and then doing so days later. 🏛️ What's next: An immediate crisis for the company is the political whirlwind it's facing on Capitol Hill, where Huang will testify twice next week — once in a nonpublic hearing and once in a public forum.

 👀 What we're watching: One immediate risk to Nvidia is if Washington responds to the crisis by cracking down on AI chip exports, which would hurt Nvidia's financial prospects.

 💸 Reality check: One thing that's probably not a big threat is shareholder lawsuits — at least in terms of the total financial penalty they could pose to a company that's worth trillions of dollars.

