 2. ANT that a shame 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo: Silas Stein/picture alliance via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Anthropic shares fell another 6.5% today, closing at $81.91 — and under their IPO price of $83, Axios' Pete Gannon writes .

 Why it matters: The stock, ticker ANT, has slumped for three straight sessions since last week's record IPO — a bad look for the company, its bankers and the broader AI industry. The big picture: Not long after Anthropic's trading debut, WSJ reported Friday that a "blame game" had started among investors who cited several execution-related culprits, ranging from valuation to the opening price to a misplayed greenshoe .

 Yes, but: Anthropic's issues could also be about Anthropic, and long-standing worries about justifying AI valuations.

 Axios' Madison Mills reported today that Anthropic's unreleased models — along with those of rival OpenAI — are becoming so powerful that many employees no longer believe they can be safely released. And of course, Jensen-gate hasn't helped either 👆. Flashback: Anthropic isn't the first IPO to stumble out of the gate, just ask Tesla, Meta and SpaceX.

 Yes, but: Investors had talked about how important it was for Anthropic to avoid a SpaceX-like wobble.

 Alas, AI's first pure play has done it in just a few days. Go deeper 

 
 
